---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-postscript";
const title = "Yotpo Email & SMS vs Postscript: Shopify recovery replacement";
const desc = "Replace the SMS lane while assigning email to an explicit owner after Yotpo retirement: migration decisions, proposed acceptance checks and incentive economics.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I still choose Yotpo Email & SMS instead of Postscript?",
    "answer": "Yotpo announced full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. Its current SMS notice says the dashboard and background services are decommissioned. This is a replacement decision."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should I verify first in Postscript?",
    "answer": "Replace the SMS lane while assigning email to an explicit owner. Use the proposed checks on this page before enabling customer messages."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I rely on a Yotpo export now?",
    "answer": "No export availability is established here. Work from retained records and confirm any remaining access with the provider. Missing permission evidence must not be converted into permission to send."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I add Sequenzy as another lifecycle layer?",
    "answer": "Only if a demonstrated integration supplies the required cart and order events, eligibility, purchase exits and suppression coordination. Adding a sender without those controls adds another queue to reconcile."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare results?",
    "answer": "Use eligible profiles, delivered messages, deduplicated orders and contribution after discounts and variable costs. Platform-attributed revenue alone does not establish incremental recovery."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Yotpo Email &amp; SMS vs Postscript for Shopify recovery</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Replace the SMS lane while assigning email to an explicit owner.</p>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>A replacement decision after retirement</h2>
      <p>Yotpo scheduled Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. Its current SMS notice confirms the dashboard and background services are decommissioned. Do not select this retired service for a new recovery setup. <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">Yotpo announcement</a>; <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">current SMS notice</a>. Checked November 24, 2026.</p>
      <p>Consider Postscript when the unresolved job is an SMS program and there is a named email owner. Replacing a combined email/SMS product with an SMS workflow alone leaves campaign email, recovery email and shared exclusions unfinished.</p>
      <h2>Start with SMS eligibility</h2>
      <p>Postscript says abandoned-cart texts require compliant opt-in before abandonment. Its current requirements page specifies one message within 48 hours of the trigger. Treat those as constraints to confirm for the program and destination, not permission to build a universal three-text escalation ladder. <a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13563943-abandoned-cart-requirements-effective-june-22-2021">Vendor documentation</a>.</p>
      <h2>Proposed acceptance checks</h2>
      <p>These are exercises to run in your own setup, not tests performed for this comparison. Record event, profile, queued message, order and exit evidence for each.</p>
      <h3>Checkout event versus consent</h3>
      <p>Prepare one test profile with documented SMS opt-in and one with a phone number but no SMS permission. Abandon checkout with each. The second must not become eligible merely because Shopify supplied a number. Retain the permission source and timestamp needed to explain eligibility.</p>
      <h3>Late event and quiet hours</h3>
      <p>Propose a test that enters near quiet hours, then inspect the queued send time and event age before release. Decide with the provider how a delayed message that exceeds the allowed abandonment window is handled. A delayed send should not silently become a fresh abandonment event.</p>
      <h3>ESP and SMS overlap</h3>
      <p>Keep email in the chosen ESP and assign one person to reconcile its queue with Postscript. Purchase after email but before SMS, then inspect both queues. Repeat after an unsubscribe and a second checkout. Do not infer cross-provider suppression from a successful test inside one channel.</p>
      <h2>Rebuild from records you actually hold</h2>
      <p>Inventory retained customer records, channel permissions, opt-outs, templates, coupon rules and event mappings. Historical purchases and a contact address do not prove current marketing permission. No available Yotpo export is promised here. Reconcile missing records before adding recipients.</p>
      <p>Assign the cart, checkout and purchase stages to named senders. Disable competing Shopify or app recovery flows before launch and inspect queued messages as well as active switches. Authenticate the sending domain, verify reply routing, and release a limited eligible segment only after the acceptance checks pass.</p>
      <h2>Check the cart and the offer together</h2>
      <p>Before launch, change a variant and quantity, remove an unavailable item and follow the return link on mobile. Confirm destination, inventory and coupon behavior. Use real expiry dates and published shipping terms; a reminder cannot resolve a payment error or make unavailable inventory purchasable.</p>
      <p>For a hypothetical $176 basket at 69% gross margin, goods cost $54.56. A 10% code removes $17.60 from revenue, leaving $158.40 revenue and $103.84 gross contribution before other costs. Full-price gross contribution is $121.44. Include the text cost and incremental orders when deciding whether that incentive is justified.</p>
      <p>Compare the same eligible cohort and observation window, deduplicate orders across senders, and include returns and message charges. If testing a no-message group, choose its size from expected traffic and detectable effect rather than copying an arbitrary percentage. Vendor attribution is useful for operations; it is not a causal lift estimate.</p>
      <h2>Budget and go-live decision</h2>
      <p>Request a current quote for the actual mailable audience, automation access, SMS destinations where relevant, usage, support and migration work. No fixed price, rating or promised recovery result is established here. Keep a rollback owner and pause sends if purchase exits, permissions or cart links fail. Reconsider the provider when the required workflow cannot be demonstrated.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
